## 4. Attribution Requirement

Unless Section 8 applies, every Output that is Publicly Distributed must include Attribution.

The Attribution must:

- clearly identify Video Forge;
- include the URL https://stravelakis.com/forge;
- be reasonably visible to the intended audience;
- accompany the Output in a manner reasonably associated with the viewing, distribution, publication, or consumption of that Output.

Examples include:

- "Made with Video Forge - https://stravelakis.com/forge"
- "Created using Video Forge - https://stravelakis.com/forge"
- "Produced with Video Forge - https://stravelakis.com/forge"

Equivalent wording is permitted.

## 5. Approved Attribution Methods

Attribution may be provided through any Approved Attribution Method published by the Licensor.

Examples may include:

- a video description;
- a prominent comment;
- a visual watermark;
- a lower-third graphic;
- an opening credit;
- a closing credit;
- a branded end card;
- a branded attribution clip;
- a webpage attribution block associated with the Output;
- any other method designated by the Licensor as an Approved Attribution Method.

The Licensor may add additional Approved Attribution Methods from time to time.

No change shall invalidate Attribution already published in compliance with a previously approved method.

## 6. Attribution Must Accompany the Output

Attribution must accompany the Output itself.

Attribution that is unlikely to be encountered by a reasonable viewer of the Output shall not satisfy this requirement.

Attribution solely within a legal notice, hidden page, repository documentation, website footer, or similar location shall not by itself satisfy this License.

The purpose of this requirement is to ensure that a reasonable viewer is able to discover that Video Forge was used.

## 7. Anti-Circumvention

A Licensee shall not:

- intentionally conceal Attribution;
- render Attribution illegible or inaccessible;
- hide Attribution solely within metadata;
- obstruct Attribution through technical means;
- remove supplied Attribution assets without replacing them with another compliant Attribution method;
- intentionally remove, disable, or obscure a Provenance Signal;
- distribute any tool, Derivative Software, or service designed to remove Attribution or Provenance Signals;
- otherwise defeat the purpose of the Attribution Requirements.

Ordinary editing, cropping, compression, or format conversion of an Output that is not intended to remove a Provenance Signal is not a breach of this Section, provided the Output still carries Attribution under Section 4.

## 8. Waivers, Separate Terms and More Permissive Licences

The Licensor may waive or reduce the Attribution Requirements in writing, including through the Tier Terms of an Access Tier.

An Output Publicly Distributed while such a waiver applied remains compliant after the waiver ends, as to that same publication.

Any Licensee who needs terms more permissive than this License, including different Attribution, may contact the Licensor through https://stravelakis.com/forge to request a separate licence. The Licensor may grant or refuse such a request at its discretion.

Nothing in this License limits the Licensor's right to offer the Software or Extra Content under other terms. Where the Licensor and a Licensee have signed a separate written agreement covering the Software, that agreement governs to the extent it conflicts with this License.

## 9. Agencies, Freelancers, Contractors and Client Work

A Licensee may use the Software in connection with services performed for clients, customers, employers, production companies, agencies, contractors, or other third parties.

Where Outputs are provided to third parties for publication, the Licensee shall notify such recipients of the Attribution Requirements and shall include an appropriate contractual obligation requiring preservation of Attribution whenever reasonably practicable.

A Licensee shall not be considered in breach solely because an independent third party subsequently removes Attribution without the Licensee's knowledge, authorization, or participation, provided that:

- the Licensee fulfilled its obligations under this Section;
- the Licensee did not encourage or request removal of Attribution; and
