## 16. Notice and Opportunity to Cure

The primary purpose of enforcement under this License is to secure compliance with the Attribution Requirements.

Except in cases of Serious Misuse, before pursuing legal remedies for an alleged violation, the Licensor shall provide written notice describing the alleged breach.

The Licensee shall have thirty (30) calendar days from receipt of such notice to cure the breach.

A cure may include:

- adding missing Attribution;
- restoring removed Attribution;
- replacing non-compliant Attribution with compliant Attribution;
- correcting another violation identified in the notice.

No damages may be sought under Section 19 where the Licensee fully cures the violation within the cure period. A cure does not affect the allocation of arbitration costs under Section 20.

Serious Misuse cannot be cured and is not subject to this Section.

## 17. Good Faith Enforcement

The parties acknowledge that the purpose of this License is to obtain Attribution in exchange for use of the Software.

Enforcement of this License is intended to secure compliance and compensation for uncured breaches and not to interfere with a Licensee's lawful livelihood, business, clients, publications, audience, or revenue streams.

The Licensor shall act reasonably and in good faith when providing notice of alleged violations.

## 18. Termination

If a material breach is not cured within the thirty (30) day cure period, the Licensor may terminate this License.

This License and all rights granted to the Licensee terminate automatically, without notice or cure period, if the Licensee:

- commits Serious Misuse; or
- starts litigation or other proceedings (including a counterclaim) against the Licensor alleging that the Software or any Output infringes the Licensee's intellectual property or other rights, as of the date those proceedings start.

Upon termination, further use of the Software, including the creation of new Outputs, shall be unauthorized, and the Licensee shall delete its copies of the Software and Derivative Software.

Outputs Publicly Distributed in compliance with this License before termination may remain published, except Outputs involving Serious Misuse. Outputs that are not compliant must be brought into compliance or withdrawn.

The rights and obligations arising before termination shall survive to the extent permitted by applicable law.

## 19. Compensation for Uncured Attribution Violations

No compensation may be sought for an Attribution violation that is cured within the cure period described in Section 16.

Where:

- Attribution was required under this License;
- the Licensee received proper notice;
- the violation was not cured within the cure period; and
- the Output remains publicly distributed without required Attribution,

the Licensor may seek compensation.

Compensation for any single violation shall not exceed €1,000.

For purposes of this Section, each separate publication of an Output at a distinct URL or location controlled by the Licensee constitutes a separate potential violation. Views, downloads, and copies made by independent third parties are not separate violations.

Nothing in this Section limits a court's or arbitral tribunal's ability to refuse, reduce, or otherwise adjust any award in accordance with applicable law.

## 20. Mandatory Arbitration

Any dispute arising out of or relating to this License, including its existence, validity, breach, or termination, shall be finally resolved by binding arbitration, and not in court, except as stated in this Section.

The arbitration shall be conducted under the UNCITRAL Arbitration Rules in force when the arbitration starts. There shall be one arbitrator. If the parties do not agree on the arbitrator within thirty (30) days after the arbitration starts, the appointing authority shall be designated by the Secretary-General of the Permanent Court of Arbitration. The seat of arbitration shall be Athens, Greece. The language of the arbitration shall be English or Greek. The proceedings may be held by video conference.

**Costs.** If the arbitral tribunal finds that the Licensee committed any breach of this License, whether minor or major, and whether or not it was later cured, the Licensee shall bear all fees and costs of the arbitration, including the fees of the arbitral institution and of the arbitrator. In all other cases, the party that loses the arbitration shall bear those fees and costs. Where each party succeeds in part, the tribunal shall divide those fees and costs in proportion to each party's success.

Either party may seek urgent interim relief, such as an order to restore Attribution or to stop Serious Misuse, from a competent court pending the arbitration.

Where mandatory law gives a consumer the right to bring or defend proceedings in the courts of the consumer's country of habitual residence, or makes this Section or any part of it non-binding on a consumer, this Section applies to that consumer only to the extent that law allows.

